      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sourdough starters thrive on a symbiotic relationship between wild yeasts and lactic acid bacteria (LAB). The yeasts consume sugars in the flour and produce carbon dioxide, which creates the leavening and rise. Simultaneously, the LAB ferment sugars into lactic and acetic acids, which are responsible for that characteristic tangy, sour flavor. This combined metabolic activity is what gives sourdough its unique texture and taste.",
